Tag every pointer event with its device and whether it was synthesized from touch.

What: `TouchEvent` gets a `device: PointerDevice` field (Mouse, Touch, Pen). The new `Window::pointer_source()`
returns a `PointerSource { device, synthesized }` for the pointer event being dispatched, and `None` otherwise (key
events, render, after dispatch returns). Render sees `None` even for the draw a tap runs before its dispatch returns.
`dispatch_event` sets it from a table with one arm per variant (`pointer_source`):
- backend mouse, scroll, pinch and file-drop input is `Mouse`, not synthesized;
- a raw touch is its own device, not synthesized;
- a back gesture, and the platform's long press and touch drag (which name no touch), are `Touch`, synthesized.
Every tap, pan and long press that the core recognizer builds from a touch carries that touch's device, synthesized:
the recognizer's per-touch state (`ActiveTouch`, and `Momentum` for the fling it leaves) records the device, so a pen
and a finger down together each label their own gestures. Momentum keeps the device of the touch that flung, up to
its closing scroll, even when a different touch catches the fling. The value is saved and restored around each
dispatch, so neither a nested dispatch nor a timer-driven gesture (long press, momentum) can leave it stale. iOS
reads `UITouch.type`: Pencil is `Pen`, an iPad pointer's indirect touch is `Mouse`. Web touches are `Touch`.

Why: GPUI turns taps into mouse presses and pans into scroll events, so a listener cannot tell a finger from a mouse.
The only signal is the window-wide `last_input_was_touch()`. Platform behaviour (touch targets, selection handles,
hover) depends on the device behind the event in hand. A field on every mouse and scroll event would touch ~95 struct
literals across all backends; a per-dispatch query carries the same information without changing those types.

Known limit: web pens take the mouse path (`pointerType == "pen"` is not a touch), so they read as `Mouse`. Only a
device field on mouse events could carry that.

Test: `pointer_source_names_the_device_and_synthesis_of_each_event` (window.rs), one row per input kind (pen and
finger down together, a finger after a pen, a finger catching a pen's fling, a back gesture); each row also records
what render sees.

Size: ~745 lines, ~350 added, ~215 of those the test. Kept as one patch past the ~200-line rule by owner exception
(2026-11-26): the new `TouchEvent::device` field breaks every struct literal of its type (core tests, iOS, web), so
the type and the literal updates land together to compile, and the window query is what reads it. The iOS mapping is
~20 lines, the recognizer's per-touch device ~40.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

